## Implementation Notes

- Grass source inputs use the validated 3-step chain: Custom HLSL input -> `Module.*` ParameterMapGet -> `User.RS_Grass_*`.
- Array DI links were verified by `inspect_niagara_data_interface_overrides`: `Module.Positions -> User.RS_Grass_Positions` and `Module.Velocities -> User.RS_Grass_Velocities`, each with one override link.
- Scalar links are present in graph inspection for `User.RS_Grass_Active`, `WindowOriginWS`, `WindowSizeWS`, `DefaultRadius`, `DefaultStrength`, and `Persistence`.
- The runner uses split PIE commands with real editor-frame waits. A single synchronous `execute_python` command can produce a false zero RT because it blocks render/GPU simulation frames.
- The direction/V-flip smoke fixes runtime `User.RS_Grass_Persistence` to `0.0` so each direction is a single-stamp test and previous Grid2D content cannot bleed into the next direction.
- Controller has Grass namespace/source variables and Phase 1 shell functions. Full controller-side source collection, compaction, and Niagara push graph remains Phase 3 per implementation plan.

## Unsure Decisions

- User visual gate is still required for whether the Grass response looks good in the actual foliage materials.
- Phase 3 must prove the controller BP graph path, not just direct Niagara component runtime push.
- Phase 6 must prove Ground+Grass simultaneous activation with isolated RT/User namespaces.

## Tooling Note

No project C++ was used. No UnrealMCP C++ change was required for Phase 2. The earlier RT zero result was a harness issue: spawn/push/readback happened inside one synchronous Python command.
